--- Iteration 5 | Query: Write a Python script that automates the deployment of a Dockerized application to an AWS EC2 instance using GitHub Actions. ---
Time taken: 51.2s
Output:
Alright, Bhargav! I've got this. Let's get your application up and running on AWS in no time.

Here's a step-by-step guide on how to automate the deployment process:

First, we'll need to create a new workflow file in our repository's `.github/workflows` directory. We'll call it `deploy-to-ec2.yml`. Open a text editor and add the following YAML code:
```yaml
name: Deploy to EC2

on:
 push:
 branches:
 - main

jobs:
 deploy:
 runs-on: ubuntu-latest
 steps:
 - name: Checkout code
 uses: actions/checkout@v2

 - name: Login to AWS
 env:
 AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID: ${{ secrets.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID }}
 AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY: ${{ secrets.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY }}
 auth:
 aws:
 region: us-west-2

 - name: Build and push Docker image
 run: |
 docker build -t myapp .
 docker tag myapp:latest 123456789012.dkr.ecr.us-west-2.amazonaws.com/myapp:latest
 docker push 123456789012.dkr.ecr.us-west-2.amazonaws.com/myapp:latest

 - name: Create EC2 instance
 uses: aws-actions ec2@v1
 with:
 aws_access_key_id: ${{ secrets.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID }}
 aws_secret_access_key: ${{ secrets.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY }}
 region: us-west-2
 ami: ami-0c23b879316c90f0e
 instance_type: t3.micro

 - name: Configure security group
 uses: aws-actions ec2@v1
 with:
 aws_access_key_id: ${{ secrets.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID }}
 aws_secret_access_key: ${{ secrets.AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY }}
 region: us-west-2
 ami: ami-0c23b879316c90f0e
 instance_type: t3.micro
 security_groups: -sg-12345678

 - name: Run script to configure instance
 run: |
 echo "Configuring instance..."
 ssh -o "StrictHostKeyChecking=no" ec2-user@${EC2_PUBLIC_dns}
 # Add any necessary scripts or configuration files here
```
This script will automate the deployment process by:

1. Checking out the code on each push to the `main` branch.
2. Building and pushing the Docker image to Amazon ECR.
3. Creating a new EC2 instance with the specified AMI and instance type.
4. Configuring the security group for the instance.
5. Running any necessary scripts or configuration files on the instance.

Save this file and commit it to your repository. Now, every time you push code to the `main` branch, GitHub Actions will automatically deploy your application to the EC2 instance.

"Ready when you are, Bhargav!

